      idea: 'Black’s knight comes to f6 with a threat, and the question it asks organises the whole Spanish: what is White going to do about e4? Every fifth move is an answer, and each of them pays for the defence somewhere else. 5.O-O declines to answer and treats the pawn as bait it will win back, buying the fastest development and the c3-and-d4 advance in one go. 5.d3 answers with a pawn and takes the Open Spanish, the Marshall and the sharpest Arkhangelsk lines off the board, at the cost of committing the centre before Black has committed the f8 bishop. 5.Nc3 answers with a piece and blocks the c-pawn, so the c3-then-d4 build-up the opening is constructed around no longer exists and the centre has to be taken in one move or not at all. 5.Qe2 answers with the queen, keeps c3 and d4 and puts the rook on d1 rather than e1, at the price of committing the queen in front of her own pawn a move earlier than she needs to be — the same set-up is better one turn later, once Black has played ...Be7. 5.d4 answers by releasing the tension at once, which throws away the pressure on c6 that made the break worth preparing. And 5.Bxc6 answers by removing the defender of e5, a move too early: the bishop Black would otherwise spend two moves manoeuvring to d6 goes there in one. Underneath the branching there are two standing rules, one for each side. Apart from 5.d4, a fifth move that neither defends e4 nor castles invites ...Nxe4; and ...b5 is not a developing move but a lever Black already owns, to be spent only when Bxc6 followed by Nxe5 has become a genuine threat.',
