      idea: 'This is the crossroads of the whole ...Nc6 and ...e5 family, and what is being decided here is a move order rather than a structure. Black kicks the d4 knight before committing the king’s knight, keeping ...g6, ...Bg7 and ...Nge7 available so the thematic ...f5 can arrive quickly and the knight is never pinned on f6 by Bg5. The cost is precise and is the reason the move is treated as slightly inferior to 4...Nf6: with no black knight on f6, e4 is not attacked, so White’s queen’s knight is not dragged to c3 in front of the c-pawn. That single freedom hands White the c2-c4 bind and the long knight tour to d5 in a version the Sveshnikov never permits. White’s reply is forced in the sense that only 5.Nb5 tests anything: the knight heads for the d6 hole that ...e5 has just created. Black must then choose. 5...d6 blocks the square and enters the Kalashnikov, taking on a backward pawn and a hole in exchange for the d4 square. 5...a6 is the Löwenthal proper and invites 6.Nd6+ Bxd6 7.Qxd6, giving up the dark-squared bishop outright to gain time on the queen; the position is unusual — White’s knight made four of his first seven moves — and Black is playing to develop with tempo, land a knight on d4 or b4 and strike with ...d5. Theory holds it dubious rather than unsound. 5...Nf6 is the move to avoid, because it invites the Sveshnikov in a version White has improved: 6.N1c3 or the immediate 6.Bg5 arrive before Black is ready, and the knight should simply have gone to f6 on move four.',
