      idea: 'Both 1...c6 and 1...e6 prepare ...d5; the difference is which piece pays for it. After ...e6 the light-squared bishop is shut in for the rest of the game, and after ...c6 it is not — that single fact is the whole reason the Caro-Kann exists, and it is why ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 comes before ...e6 in nearly every line rather than after it. The cost is a tempo, and it is charged twice: the freeing break in these structures is ...c5, so the c-pawn has to travel c7-c6-c5 while a French player’s goes to c5 in one move. Black cannot outrun that, so he does not try. He develops first, keeps the position closed enough that the lost time does not tell, and plays ...c5 once, late, when every piece is ready to use the open lines it creates. White’s third move chooses which game this becomes, and the four choices are genuinely different openings rather than shades of one: 3.e5 takes space and hunts the bishop that came out; 3.exd5 fixes a Carlsbad structure and plays on both wings; 3.Nc3 or 3.Nd2 invites ...dxe4 and a small lead in development against a position with no weaknesses; 3.c4 changes the subject to an isolated queen’s pawn. What survives all four is Black’s claim: no target, a healthy pawn structure, a bishop that got out, and an endgame that is at least as good as White’s.',
