      idea: 'The Carlsbad structure with the colours the other way round from the Exchange Queen’s Gambit, and the reversal is the whole point: it is Black who has no c-pawn here, so it is Black who owns the minority attack with ...b5-b4, and White who plays in the centre and on the kingside. Unlike the French and Slav Exchange Variations, nothing has been opened — there is no file for the rooks to trade down on, which is why this is not the drawish line its name suggests. What gives White a real question to ask is the move order. 4.Bd3 is played before anything is developed for one reason: it takes f5 from the bishop the whole Caro-Kann was played to free. Add c3 and h3 and ...Bg4 is gone as well, and Black is left choosing between shutting the bishop in with ...e6 after all, or ...g6 and ...Bf5, which solves the bishop at the cost of the plan — a bishop on g7 contributes nothing to ...b5-b4, where one on d6 would cover b4 and c5 and support the whole operation. That trade-off is the variation. Black’s cleanest answer is the break ...e5, accepting an isolated d-pawn in return for free pieces and no long-term worries; it is unpopular mainly because players who choose the Caro-Kann tend not to want an isolani.',
