      idea: 'Black recaptures with a piece rather than the queen, or does not recapture at all, and the first real decision is White’s: hold the extra pawn or take the centre. Holding it with 3.c4 is the move Black is hoping for. The pawn cannot be kept in any case — ...c6 and ...e6 come with tempo against a structure White has not had time to support — and the gambit that follows gives Black the easier development, the d4 square and a genuine initiative for material White will give back anyway. 3.d4 answers the question the other way: the pawn is offered back on White’s terms, and what remains is two centre pawns against a knight that has moved twice and a queen still at home. From here the position splits into three different openings. 3...Nxd5 is the main road and produces a game closer to the Alekhine than the Scandinavian, where White gains time on the knight with c4 and Black must find squares for a piece that keeps being asked to move. 3...Bg4 is the Portuguese, a real gambit in which Black gives the pawn for good in return for the pin and the fastest development in the opening. And 3...Qxd5 simply transposes to the ordinary queen recapture, which is a small admission: the knight move was inserted for free and gave White extra options in return for nothing.',
