      idea: 'The same two pawn moves as the main line in the other order, and the order is the whole content of the position. White intends c2-c4 in the Four Pawns Attack and the Exchange Variation anyway, so playing it before d2-d4 costs nothing — and it buys the removal of two of Black’s better ideas, both of which depend on White having committed the d-pawn first. After 3.d4 Black can play 3...Nb6, taking the knight to the square c4 would drive it to and spending the tempo saved on ...d5: a French Defence in which the light-squared bishop is outside the chain. He can also play the odd 3...c6, intending to tuck the knight away on c7 rather than have it hunted to the edge. Neither is available here, because the knight is being asked to move at once and it has nowhere better than b6. What White gives up in exchange is the immediate grip on d4 — the pawn is still at home, so Black’s counterplay against the centre starts one move earlier than usual — and the fact that after 3...Nb6 White is now more or less obliged to follow up with d4, which is what he wanted anyway. Objectively the two move orders are worth about the same; the reason to prefer this one is that it narrows the game to lines White has actually studied.',
