      idea: 'The pawn arrives on d4 already defended by the queen, and that one difference sets the tone for everything that follows: the centre cannot be knocked over by a tactic, so the games are slower, the plans matter more than the calculation, and the theory burden is lighter than after 1.e4. What the move does not yet do is fight for e4, and that square is what the whole opening is about. White’s natural way of contesting it is c4, adding a second attacker to d5 and opening the queenside; almost every 1.d4 repertoire either plays it at once or is deliberately built around delaying it. Black chooses between meeting the pawn head-on with 1...d5, which stops e4 immediately and leads to the Queen’s Gambit complex, and the Indian defences, which concede the big centre for a move or two and attack it with pieces and a well-timed ...c5 or ...e5.',
