      idea: 'The c-pawn holds d5 exactly as ...e6 would in the Queen’s Gambit Declined, and it leaves the light-squared bishop’s diagonal open — that bishop, and getting it to f5 or g4 before ...e6 shuts it in, is the entire purpose of the defence. The complication is that ...c6 does not defend b7, so the instant the bishop steps off c8 the queen check from b3 hits b7 and d5 at the same time. Almost every move-order refinement in the Slav is an answer to that one problem, and the main line answers it by capturing on c4 first: with no white pawn on c4 the queen sortie no longer forks anything, so ...Bf5 follows in comfort, and from f5 the bishop sits on the square White most wants for a pawn. What Black pays is a tempo — the c-pawn has to travel to c5 in two moves rather than one — and White’s whole game consists of making that tempo tell, almost always by getting e4 in before the black pieces are ready for it.',
