        'Answer ...dxc4 and ...b5 with a4. It stops the pawn being held, at the cost of a hole on b4 that stays there for the rest of the game.',
        'Exchange on d5 and play the symmetrical position. It is dry and it is not a draw: with thirty pieces still on, the extra tempo is real, and White is happiest making the trade the moment Black touches the c8 bishop or puts a knight on d7.',
        'Play 3.Nc3 and 4.e3, which rules out ...Bf5 and ...Bg4 altogether and leaves Black with the Semi-Slav or something worse.',
        'Keep Qb3 in reserve. It is the standing threat against every scheme in which the bishop has left home and b7 is undefended.',
