      idea: 'Black hands over the centre deliberately, on the understanding that a centre is only an asset while it is intact. The bishop on g7 does the work: it looks straight down the long diagonal at d4 and e4, so White cannot leave the pawns where they are — the moment Black exchanges on d4 the bishop comes alive and the pawn on e4 has too few defenders. So White pushes d4-d5 and closes the position, which is exactly what Black was waiting for. A locked centre draws the battle lines: Black’s pawns on d6 and e5 point at White’s king, White’s on c4 and d5 point at Black’s queen, and each side attacks in the direction his own pawns are facing. The stakes are not symmetrical, which is the practical heart of the opening — White breaking through on the queenside wins material, Black breaking through on the kingside wins the game, and that asymmetry is worth more in a real game than the evaluation suggests. Where White refuses the classical shape, Black changes structure rather than plan: against the Sämisch set-up with f3 the break is ...c5 instead of ...e5, and the game becomes a Benoni in which White’s king has trouble finding a home behind his own f-pawn. Underneath all of it is a fight for the dark squares — the colour of the fianchettoed bishop, of the pawns on d6 and e5, and of every square the attack travels over.',
