      idea: 'White answers a fianchetto with a fianchetto, and the point is defensive as much as it is constructive. The bishop on g2 contests e4 and d5 from a distance, but its more important job is that it, and the knight on f3 behind it, garrison the squares Black’s King’s Indian attack has to break through — the ...f5-f4 and ...g5-g4 storm that decides the Classical simply does not arrive here, because the king it is aimed at has more defenders than in any other version. So Black rarely persists with the standard plan and instead chooses a different game entirely, and this move is where that choice is made: ...d6 and ...c5 for a Fianchetto Benoni, ...d5 for the Grünfeld treatment, ...c6 and ...d5 for a Slav-like wall. The order matters as much as the choice. White has played g3 before Nf3, so c4 is undefended, and Black’s ...d5 and ...dxc4 is available in a way it is not once the knight has come out.',
