      idea: 'The pin is not an end in itself; it is the cheapest way to stop e2-e4. White has both centre pawns available and would like to build the big centre, and pinning the piece that defends e4 postpones it for as long as the pin lasts. From that single idea the whole opening unfolds as a trade: Black will usually take on c3 at some point, and the price of the bishop pair is a structural concession White cannot repair — after bxc3 the c-pawns are doubled and the front one on c4 is fixed on a light square where no pawn can ever defend it. So the argument is static structure against dynamic activity, and it is decided by whether the position opens. If White gets the centre moving, the two bishops on open diagonals are worth more than the damaged queenside; if Black keeps the position closed and blockades, the weak pawn simply stays weak. Everything White plays on move four is a different answer to the same question — whether to allow the doubling, avoid it with a queen or bishop move, or invite it and claim the centre is compensation.',
