      idea: 'The whole point is what is missing. With no knight on c3 there is nothing for ...Bb4 to pin, so the Nimzo-Indian — the line most 1.d4 players least want to face — is off the board, and Black has to choose a different game. The choice is the content of this position. 3...d5 goes back into Queen’s Gambit and Catalan territory. 3...b6 is the Queen’s Indian, which is precisely what 3.Nf3 concedes in exchange. 3...c5 4.d5 is a Benoni, but a Benoni in which White has already committed the knight to f3 and therefore cannot play the pawn-storm systems that make the opening frightening — a well-known trade in the other direction, since many players steer for exactly this. 3...Bb4+ is the Bogo-Indian, asking the Nimzo’s question without the knight there to answer it. The move order also has to be compared with 3.g3, and the comparison runs both ways: 3.g3 keeps the Queen’s Indian out but commits the bishop early, so 3...c5 gives Black a good Benoni in which the bishop on g2 has little to do, and 3...Bb4+ arrives with g3 already played. 3.Nf3 keeps the fianchetto in reserve until Black has shown his hand, which is why it is the standard Catalan move order as well as the anti-Nimzo one.',
