      idea: 'The bishop comes out before the knight, and the entire point is the square the knight then gets. In the Exchange Variation White’s natural developing move is Nf3, and it is met by ...Bg4, pinning the piece that defends d4 at the moment Black adds ...Nc6 and ...cxd4 to the position — the standing structural problem of White’s whole set-up. Playing Bc4 first means the knight goes to e2 instead, where ...Bg4 is answered by f3 and the pin is nothing, and where the f-pawn is no longer blocked and can go to f4 and f5 once the dark-squared bishops come off. The formation Bc4, Ne2, Be3, O-O is as solid as the centre ever gets: it defends d4 with two pieces and takes the sting out of both ...Bg4 and ...Qa5, the two standard ways of getting at it. What White pays is activity — the pieces are compact rather than dangerous — and one target, because the bishop on c4 is exposed to ...Na5 and to the pressure from a bishop on b7, and it frequently has to spend a move retreating to d3. The game that follows is the oldest argument in the Grünfeld: White has the big centre and, as Steinitz’s rule requires, must play actively to prove it is worth anything, and the activity creates the weaknesses that Black’s counterattack lives on. Every world champion from Botvinnik onward has taken a side.',
