      idea: 'Black offers a pawn on the second move to blow up the centre before it is built, and the offer is genuine only for a move: the pawn is unsupported, White should take it, and in the main line Black comes straight back for it with the knight. What is really being bought is time and space for the minor pieces — the pawn on d4 disappears, the dark squares open, and a bishop on c5 or b4 starts pressing before White has developed anything. The other half of its appeal is not on the board at all. The gambit costs Black almost nothing to prepare, which makes it a standing nuisance to a 1.d4 player who has no fixed answer, and that practical asymmetry is the honest reason it still gets played. Objectively the modern verdict is settled and unflattering: Black does not equalise, and every serious repertoire treats the line as something to know rather than something to fear. The choice that decides the game is White’s third and fourth moves, and it is a choice between two attitudes rather than a list of variations. He can hold the extra pawn, which is objectively best and requires real analysis and some risk of getting it wrong; or he can hand it straight back and take a large space advantage and a lead in development in exchange, which is what most modern courses recommend precisely because it needs no memory. Black’s own fork is between 3...Ng4, the main line, which comes back for the pawn, and 3...Ne4, the Fajarowicz, which does not.',
