      idea: 'The knight turns round for the pawn and White has to decide what the extra pawn is worth to him. The courses that cover this position all sort White’s replies the same way, into two concepts rather than four moves. The first is to defend e5 and keep the material: Bf4 is the accurate way to do it, and Nf3 is the inaccurate one, because after Nf3 Black has ...Bc5 — the bishop lands on the diagonal with tempo, White is more or less forced into e3, the option of Bf4 has been spent, and ...Nc6 and ...Qe7 recover the pawn anyway. Even the accurate version has a cost: Black gets ...f6 in, the pawn comes back, and the positions are the kind that are pleasant for White and awkward to actually win. The second concept is to hand the pawn straight back and charge Black for the tempi instead. Alekhine’s e4 is the old version — take the whole centre, and let the knight come and collect while White develops behind the pawns. The modern version is the quieter e3, which returns the pawn too but plays for a grip on d5 and a space advantage rather than a big centre; its practical point is that Black finds he cannot advance any pawn to the fifth rank at all, ...a5 excepted, and a position with no pawn breaks is not what a gambit was played for. Everything else is worse for a reason worth knowing: the diagonal the g1 knight and the f-pawn keep opening is the one Black’s bishop is aimed down.',
