      idea: 'Black leaves the knight on e4 hanging and develops instead, and the offer is not an offer at all. Bxe4 is answered by ...d5, which forks the bishop and the pawn on d4 and, after the bishop steps back, ...e4 forks it again together with the knight on f3; d5 driving the c6 knight away is met by ...Nf6 and the same ...e4 push, so the piece comes back in every version. What Black has really done with the tempo is contest d4 while White’s pieces are still on the wrong squares, and the variation carries the names of Murey, who introduced it, and of the modern Petrov specialists who revived it. The strategic objection is quieter and is the reason it has never become the main line: c6 is not where Black’s queen’s knight belongs in this opening. The standard Petrov scheme is ...Nc5, ...Be7, castling, and then ...c6 and ...Nd7, where the knight supports the centre and challenges anything White puts on e5 — and a knight already on c6 blocks the pawn that would hold d5 and stands on a square where Nxc6 wrecks the structure. So the line is a tactical device that buys a tempo now and costs a plan later, and White’s job is to reach a position where the tactics have finished and the misplaced knight is still there.',
