      idea: 'The point of no return. Up to here White still had quiet options — Bd3 and Nc3 both keep the game in Schmidt Scotch territory — and 6.e5 abandons all of them for a direct attack on the knight. What follows is close to forced for a long way, which is the real reason the line is played: it is the sharpest thing White has against 1...e5 and the most forcing. The bargain is easy to state. White has more space and by far the better structure, since Black’s c-pawns are doubled and his a-pawn will often end up isolated as well, so every trade into an endgame favours White. Black has a lead in development that grows with every knight move White has already spent, and the pawn on e5 that started all this is chronically difficult to defend. The stronger the opposition, the more likely this is the move: it appears in most master games with the Scotch and in a minority of online ones, because the alternatives are easier to play and worse.',
